okay so last week cs go fans everywhere  feasted their eyes on an announcement  that  quite frankly they never thought they  would valve has made it so that  vac bans are temporary well  certain ones not all of them but still  it's something  after years of begging and pleading the  iron curtain  has been lifted with valve finally  showing some  modicum of mercy when it comes to  rehabilitating cheaters  and what's even cooler to think about is  what this could spell for the future  of counter-strike  alright you all know the drill like the  video slap  the subscribe button and turn on  notifications so that  hopefully when kenny finally makes his  way onto a roster  you get to watch the video that i make  about it straight after it goes live  dearly beloved cs go fans we are  gathered here today because last  thursday valve published a stunning  unexpected and altogether  earth-shattering blog post  in which they decreed that certain vac  bans  would no longer be permanent but first  things first  what is vac and why are the bands that  it engenders  feared across the whole of esports  remember guys  league players watch these videos too  vac which stands for valve anti-cheat is  the official anti-cheat of valve  published multiplayer titles  such as dota 2 team fortress 2 and cs go  thanks dmitry it's automated which means  it triggers without human intervention  it's also egregiously awful like  only catches the free cheats awful most  spin modding silvers square up against  vac on the regular  and don't even break a sweat don't  believe me  just ask that [ __ ] who openly bragged  about his cheats in a video with  sparkles last august  i don't wake up in the morning wondering  if i've been banned  even after a really cheeky couple games  because  at this point i i just can't see how i'm  gonna get banned also  fun fact apparently that kid's account  did get banned  like days after the interview went live  i guess that's what happens when you  publicly thumb your nose at the cs go  devs  here's the thing though for years it has  been the case that if you have any hope  of playing counter-strike  competitively and you were at any point  in your life  associated with a vac banned account and  it was deserved  not a bug you were in for a [ __ ]  world of hurt see the thing about vac  bans wasn't just that they were  permanent  it's that if deemed legit they were  literally impossible to reverse  if at any point your name had been  associated with a vac banned account  that's it you were done prohibited from  participating in  any valve sponsored events i.e the  majors  for life even if the infraction had been  committed  before your time as a professional  player let's say for instance that you  are a 13 year old  [ __ ] who installs aim hacks to troll  people in matchmaking  let's also say that that 13 year old  blossoms into a world world-class  competitor  who's desperately trying to crack into  the upper echelon of competitive  counter-strike the thing is  they can't because according to valve  they are  were and always will be a cheater  now that's essentially what happened to  brazilian prodigy  vsm whose story we covered back in  december  remember if they all of a sudden make an  exception for vsm  that would open up the floodgates for  all of the other cheaters they've  blacklisted over the years  if vsm walks free why not yampy why not  brax or the rest of the eye by power  guys  where does valve draw the line don't get  me wrong i am all for sending cheaters  to the [ __ ]  shadow realm but to destroy a guy's  career before it even gets started  because of some smooth brain [ __ ] he did  when he was a pre-teen  that's just silly an even crazier thing  happened to finnish phenom yampi whose  story we covered last  april after eating a vac ban back in  2015  on an account that he claims he wasn't  even playing on  yampi was banned from participating in  any and all  valve sponsored events he even went so  far as to sue the developer over damages  that he claimed he incurred  whilst being unable to play in the  majors and ultimately  that's the problem with being a  professional cs go competitor and  having one of these vac bans hanging  over your head  nobody wants to sign you remember if you  can't compete in valve events then your  roster will either have to field a sixth  player  just for the majors which can screw up  your qualification points  or just forfeit playing them entirely  and if you do that then you're also  forfeiting the prestige of competing in  such an event  and the profit that comes with sticker  revenue thus for literally the entirety  of cs go's lifespan  a vac ban has stood as the equivalent of  a death sentence a surefire way to  either end your career  or turn it into a non-starter that is  until last week when out of nowhere  valve made this announcement  in valve's own words a vac ban will only  disqualify a player from an event if it  was either received less than five years  prior  or if it was received at any time after  their first participation in a valve  sponsored event  eg after participating in a qualifier  for an rmr event  so what does this mean well the first  part is pretty self-explanatory  if your vac ban is more than five years  old it will no longer prohibit you from  competing in valve sponsored events  simple the exception of course is if you  received your vac ban at  any point after participating in a valve  sponsored event  that's what the second part says in  other words if you have ever competed in  anything that is even remotely related  to cs go's regional major rankings  and you're caught cheating you're for  life  the most infamous example of this is  probably cali  a tier one french professional who ate a  vac ban  whilst playing for titan just one week  before dreamhack winter 2014.  now to be fair that does seem fairly  justified  if you are a top tier professional  competing on the biggest stage  in the world and you're caught cheating  you probably don't deserve a second  chance  it's not like you were some 13 year old  kid cheating in matchmaking who can  claim that they didn't know any better  then again community members have been  clamoring for years that valve makes  some effort at rehabilitating the i buy  power crew  and they too were salaried professionals  look  it may not feel to you as if match  fixing is on par with using aim hacks  but it's still cheating oh and by the  way if you were wondering what this  announcement might mean for the i buy  power boys  the answer is nothing valve made sure to  note that there are other reasons a  player may not be eligible to  participate in valve sponsored events  these remain unchanged so who does this  help  well it sure as [ __ ] helps vsm who  tweeted out a screenshot of him  uninstalling valorant the same day this  announcement went live  it also isn't terrible for yampi who  after months of litigating with valve to  no avail  made the decision to step down from ends  to join the ranks of team liquid's  valorante roster  of course now that he's been unbanned  everyone in the cs community is  pestering him as to whether he'll ever  consider switching back  i know everyone's asking will i come  back to csun  i can't not just i'm not gonna even  think about it at least in a week  you know we have a long qualifiers i  want to  win the qualifiers and everything and  ultimately that's what makes this  announcement as exciting as it is  the possibilities it opens up finally  after years of begging and pleading with  valve to be more  attentive adaptive and sympathetic to  csgo's competitive community  the developer has shown that it is  capable of  showing mercy as for why valve all of a  sudden decided to do this now  let's be honest it probably has  something to do with valorant  and the fact that we are losing players  left and right that said the start of  kovid marked global offensive's  biggest year to date and for as much as  people love to meme on it for being a  dead game it's actually more popular now  than it's ever been  and as psyched as i am to see vsm  finally make his way onto a real roster  and to potentially get yampi back it's  what this announcement spells for the  future  that excites me the most this could  affect literally countless  young aspiring competitors who we've yet  to even hear of  kids who were either stupid enough to  cheat or  like yampy had their name attached to an  account that was  somehow later plagued with cheats in  either case they won't need to be as  fearful for their future and can take  comfort in the fact that  as it turns out valve is actually  capable of being reasoned with  just please for the love of god don't  think that it's okay  to just suddenly start cheating just  because you're 13.  oh look man we've all been there we've  all been there i was playing retakes  last week and there was a kid  who was literally cheating in the  community server retakes  just like spin body in community servant  retakes like could you imagine  there's no elo on the line like what are  you doing like what why are you here  what are you like are you practicing  your cheats  that's got to be it right  he's practicing his toggling the best  part is that i queued with him later on  in a game  like a separate day on a separate server  and he wasn't cheated so i was just like  this kid was probably practicing his  toggles  it's just so crazy that like i played  with him like a week later  in a different server different day he  was on voice he was on his mic  and i was like oh you're that kid who  cheats and retakes and he got like all  sheepish and i was just like i can't  believe this
 

